; DOMAIN: geometry/curvature-and-transport [CLOSED, derived 2026-07-26]
; Claim: Ricci curvature unifies smooth flow, discrete graphs, and optimal transport. (1) Hamilton-Perelman Ricci flow d_t g=-2 Ric(g) is the gradient flow of Perelman's F-entropy F(g,f)=int (R+|grad f|^2) e^{-f} dV, monotone non-decreasing, with the W-entropy giving monotonicity along the flow. (2) Ollivier-Ricci curvature discretizes this on metric-measure/graph spaces: kappa(x,y)=1-W1(m_x,m_y)/d(x,y), comparing transport cost of local measures to base distance. (3) Lott-Sturm-Villani synthetic Ricci lower bound Ric>=K is EQUIVALENT to K-geodesic-convexity of the Boltzmann entropy H(rho)=int rho ln rho along W2 geodesics. Positive curvature = entropy is convex uphill = measures contract = faster mixing.
; Structure: (smooth Ricci flow d_t g=-2 Ric = gradient flow of Perelman F-entropy, monotone) (Ollivier kappa(x,y)=1-W1(m_x,m_y)/d(x,y) discrete curvature) (Lott-Sturm-Villani: Ric>=K <=> H(rho) is K-convex along W2 geodesics) (positive curvature => entropy convexity => contraction/mixing).
; Result: Curvature is a statement about how optimal transport distorts entropy: the Ricci lower bound is exactly the convexity modulus of Boltzmann entropy in the Wasserstein metric, so geometry (Ric), dynamics (JKO gradient flow), and information (entropy) are the same object viewed through the W2 lens. Ricci flow itself is entropy gradient descent on the space of metrics.
; Bridge: genesis_wasserstein_gradient_flow_JKO (both are entropy gradient flows in W2 — JKO evolves the measure rho by steepest entropy descent at fixed geometry, Ricci flow evolves the geometry g by Perelman-entropy descent; the LSV curvature bound Ric>=K is the K-convexity of the SAME Boltzmann entropy H(rho) whose W2 gradient flow JKO integrates) + g2741-optimal-transport (Ollivier kappa uses W1 transport cost of local measures and LSV uses W2 geodesics — curvature is defined entirely through Monge-Kantorovich transport distances) + genesis_information_geometry (Ricci/sectional curvature of the Fisher-Rao / Wasserstein statistical manifold controls estimation contraction and mixing, tying synthetic curvature to the information-geometric metric on probability measures).